Barometrik

Ingredients: OLD SKOOL JUNGLE & DRUM 'N' BASS + KRAUTROCK + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + KORG MS-10 & MS-20  
Creative Catalyst: A short-lived pirate transmitter operating out of an abandoned North Sea radar station that only fired up when barometric pressure plunged below 990 millibars.

Barometrik grafts the relentless, hypnotically steady motorik 4/4 spine of 1970s West German art-rock onto time-stretched, syncopated 160-BPM jungle breakbeats, wrapping the rhythmic crossfire in the tea-stained tape warmth of chill lo-fi and the piercing, analog squelch of vintage Korg MS series synthesizers.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 156–164 BPM. A hypnotic polyrhythmic push where an unwavering, metronomic 4/4 kick-and-snare motorik pulse anchors restless, syncopated jungle rollouts, ghost-note snares, and pitch-dropped 808 sub-booms.
- Harmonic Palette: Melancholic Dorian and Aeolian modal pads played on worn electric pianos, counterbalanced by monophonic, detuned synthesizer intervals and unresolving suspended fourths that evoke open grey water.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. MS-20 ESP Ringing: Routing chopped Amen and Think breaks through the Korg MS-20 External Signal Processor, pushing the high-pass resonance right to the brink of howling self-oscillation.
  2. Damp-Tape Flutter: Bouncing percussion sub-mixes to consumer-grade cassette decks with intentionally worn capstans, yielding warm, low-fidelity tape saturation and nautical wow-and-flutter.
  3. Barometric Gating: Sidechaining lush analog pads against natural AM-radio thunderstorm hiss so chords swell and breathe only when lightning static crackles across the spectrum.
- Dynamics: Deeply circulatory and oceanic rather than drop-driven; instead of abrasive EDM buildups, tension accumulates over long, hypnotic stretches where breakbeats softly breach beneath cozy Rhodes chords like an incoming tide.

Scene & Origin Lore
Barometrik coalesced in the late 1990s along the storm-battered dockyards of Bremerhaven and Cuxhaven, where displaced Berlin krautrock traditionalists, crate-digging Bristol jungle fanatics, and cassette-trading ambient heads took shelter in decommissioned harbor towers. Bound by maritime workwear—heavy cable-knit wool sweaters layered beneath grease-stained yellow foul-weather parkas—the youth spent high-pressure summer days trading patches and cutting breaks in drafty seaside flats. When Atlantic gale fronts pushed barometric pressure off the charts, the scene mobilized around battery-powered FM portables to tune into Radio Sturmsignal, a phantom broadcast tower perched on a salt-rotted sand spit that only transmitted during severe squalls. Gatherings inside rain-battered shipping containers turned into meditative, all-night listening rites where steaming mugs of spiced tea sat alongside smoking patch bays, locked into 160-BPM motorik transcendence while the North Sea crashed outside.

Typical Instruments: Korg MS-20 (lead squelch, screeching resonant filter sweeps, ring-mod percussion), Korg MS-10 (subterranean sine-wave subs and warm low-end drones), Fender Rhodes Mark I through tape delay, modified Roland TR-606, Akai S950 sampler (for pitch-shifted breakbeats), reel-to-reel cassette recorders, binaural hydrophones.

For Fans Of: Can, Photek, Boards of Canada, LTJ Bukem.

