Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Bollard Sings at Gale Eight
- Story & Context: When an October squall tore through the Outer Jade Channel, Hanno Vane bolted an ungrounded Korg MS-10 to the harbor master's bench while the smell of ozone and wet sheep wool filled the shack. Lightning struck the outer navigational beacon, sending a high-voltage surge through his tape deck that miraculously aligned the breakbeat loop just as the phantom station crackled to life on 91.4 FM. The resulting cut captures the exact moment the room lost power, leaving only battery-powered oscillators howling over rolling Amen breaks as Hanno whispered, "Count the flashes before the drum breaks hit."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album cover art in the style of a weathered 1970s nautical chart, depicting a cast-iron harbor bollard wrapped in fraying rope on a dark sea jetty, overlaid with minimalist synthesizer patch diagrams and faint chalk-written frequency numbers glowing under cold coastal rain.

Track 2: Did You Secure the Mooring?
- Story & Context: Recorded during a grueling twelve-hour holding pattern at Blexen Pier, Maren Seidel wrote the track’s drifting Rhodes progression while nursing bruised knuckles and tasting sea salt on her lips. A violent gust snapped the stern line of an adjacent cargo barge, sparking an argument across the docks that was captured through the studio's open vent and fed directly into the MS-20's high-pass filter. Her bittersweet vocal confession was laid down on a half-charged Walkman as the transmitter began to fade into thunderstorm static, murmuring, "Leave the hawser slack so the bass can roll."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric lyric video background showing a dimly lit harbor cabin interior at night, rain beating hard against a round porthole, with an electric piano keyboard illuminated only by the faint amber LEDs of an analog synthesizer and a half-empty mug of tea sending curls of steam into the dim light.

Track 3: Azimuth Drift on a Slack Tide
- Story & Context: Years after the pirate transmitter's antenna array finally collapsed into the mudflats of the Roter Sand shoals, veteran harbor pilot Jansen claimed the station's carrier hum was still bleeding into boat sonars during every gale. The band salvaged his crackling audio log, underpinning the distorted vocal with an unwavering, ten-minute motorik kick and a sub-bass rumble calibrated to match the engine idle of a stationary tugboat. The record closes on the hiss of receding North Sea tide foam and a faint, spectral radio voice singing, "The tide takes the signal, but the drums stay ashore."
